The gradient at the specified point is normal to the level curve. Differentiate before substituting the coordinates.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_x(x,y)&=- 2 x - 2 \\ f_y(x,y)&=2 y \\ \nabla f(1,2)&=\left(-4\right)\,i+\left(4\right)\,j \\ \|\nabla f(1,2)\|&=\sqrt{\left(-4\right)^2+\left(4\right)^2} \\ &=4 \sqrt{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

This gradient is nonzero. Divide it by its magnitude:

$$
\begin{aligned}\mathbf n&=\frac{\nabla f(1,2)}{\|\nabla f(1,2)\|} \\ &=\frac{\left(-4\right)\,i+\left(4\right)\,j}{4 \sqrt{2}} \\ &=\left(- \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right)\,i+\left(\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right)\,j
\end{aligned}
$$

Its negative is also a unit normal. Exactly one displayed choice is a unit vector parallel to the gradient.
